  "text": "We are given a quadratic polynomial:\n\n$$\np(x) = x^2 + bx + c\n$$\n\nwith real coefficients and roots $ r $ and $ s $. So, by the **Vieta's formulas**, we know:\n\n$$\nr + s = -b \\quad \\text{and} \\quad rs = c\n$$\n\nWe are told that $ p(1), p(2), p(3) $ are all **prime numbers**. So we need to find the **smallest possible value of $ r + s = -b $** under this constraint.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Express $ p(1), p(2), p(3) $\n\nWe compute:\n\n- $ p(1) = 1^2 + b(1) + c = 1 + b + c $\n- $ p(2) = 2^2 + b(2) + c = 4 + 2b + c $\n- $ p(3) = 3^2 + b(3) + c = 9 + 3b + c $\n\nWe are told these three values are all **prime**.\n\nSo:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np(1) &= 1 + b + c \\\\\np(2) &= 4 + 2b + c \\\\\np(3) &= 9 + 3b + c\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet’s denote:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np(1) &= A \\\\\np(2) &= B \\\\\np(3) &= C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= 1 + b + c \\\\\nB &= 4 + 2b + c \\\\\nC &= 9 + 3b + c\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet’s subtract these equations to eliminate $ c $:\n\n- $ B - A = 3b \\Rightarrow b = \\frac{B - A}{3} $\n- $ C - B = 3b \\Rightarrow b = \\frac{C - B}{3} $\n\nSo both expressions for $ b $ must be equal:\n\n$$\n\\frac{B - A}{3} = \\frac{C - B}{3} \\Rightarrow B - A = C - B \\Rightarrow 2B = A + C\n$$\n\nSo we must have:\n\n$$\n2B = A + C\n$$\n\nThat is a **key constraint**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Try small primes for $ A, B, C $\n\nWe are told that $ A, B, C $ are all **prime numbers**.\n\nLet’s try small primes and check if they satisfy the constraint $ 2B = A + C $.\n\nWe try:\n\n- $ A = 2, B = 3, C = 4 $ → Not all primes. $ C = 4 $ is not prime.\n- $ A = 2, B = 3, C = 5 $ → $ 2B = 6 $, $ A + C = 7 $ → Not equal.\n- $ A = 2, B = 3, C = 7 $ → $ 2B = 6 $, $ A + C = 9 $ → Not equal.\n- $ A = 2, B = 5, C = 8 $ → $ C = 8 $ not prime.\n- $ A = 3, B = 5, C = 7 $ → $ 2B = 10 $, $ A + C = 10 $ → **OK!**\n\nSo we have a valid combination:\n\n$$\nA = 3,\\quad B = 5,\\quad C = 7\n$$\n\nNow check what $ b $ and $ c $ are.\n\nFrom:\n\n$$\nA = 1 + b + c = 3 \\Rightarrow b + c = 2 \\quad \\text{(1)}\n$$\n$$\nB = 4 + 2b + c = 5 \\Rightarrow 2b + c = 1 \\quad \\text{(2)}\n$$\n\nSubtract (1) from (2):\n\n$$\n(2b + c) - (b + c) = 1 - 2 \\Rightarrow b = -1\n$$\n\nThen from (1): $ -1 + c = 2 \\Rightarrow c = 3 $\n\nSo:\n\n$$\nb = -1, \\quad c = 3\n$$\n\nThen:\n\n$$\nr + s = -b = 1\n$$\n\nSo the smallest possible value of $ r + s $ is:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{1}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### ✅ Final Answer:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{1}\n$$",
